Jakob Creutzig <creutzig@mathematik.tu-darmstadt.de> writes:

> I think the main point of a good polymorphing is to make 
> good stashes, not to have the spell ready.  With good stashes, 
> I can usually find something interesting within 3 to 4 zaps.  

"Something interesting", surely, but not "any item". I polypile quite
rarely and when I do it, it is 9 times out of 10 in Fort Ludios or in
Castle and for armor (sometimes I add useless scrolls to the pile).  I
practically always get a T- or a Hawaiian shirt but other than that
the gains are rather minimal: if I'm lucky one other usable piece of
armor (good gauntlets or a good helm) and if very lucky two pieces. 

> Well, and there are forgetful players like me who travel to 
> their stash about 10 times or more, working up to more than 
> 60k turns on average.  

There are many ways to play nethack and all are equally valid. When
people speak about usability of items they usually do it from the
viewpoint of their own playing styles. Players of different styles
have different requirements. This is one thing that should be
remembered before one starts to plan what stuff could be removed from
the game.

I believe that my playing style deviates quite far from the typical
style (no stashes, little equipment optimization) so I wanted to give
a different perspective. Also, I don't think that nethack should
necessarily be made more difficult. If someone thinks that something
makes the game too easy, then he or she should not use that feature.
For me conflict is such a thing as it makes the latter part of the
game almost trivial. So I don't use it. But I wouldn't want it to be
removed from the game, either. 

> Turn undead might be of interest sometimes, the rest appears  
> to me as the tin-openers of magic, too.  

Turn undead and create familiar might be useful, but they have so high
level that I never proceed to the point where I can really cast them.


